  "_meta": {
    "domain": "Include a person only if a historically significant part of that person's scientific or medical contribution is directly to anatomy or the structural organization of organisms; physiology and the experimental study of organ function, circulation, respiration, homeostasis or endocrine regulation; microbiology, bacteriology, virology, infectious disease or parasitology; immunology, vaccination, antibodies, cellular immunity or immune tolerance; genetics, heredity, chromosome biology, population genetics or medical genetics; biochemistry and molecular biology of enzymes, metabolism, proteins, nucleic acids, gene expression or molecular mechanisms of life; cell biology, organelles, cell division, developmental biology, stem cells or cellular differentiation; neuroscience, neuroanatomy, electrophysiology, synaptic transmission, sensory systems, learning or memory; pathology, carcinogenesis and mechanisms of disease; epidemiology, occupational medicine and scientifically grounded public health; pharmacology, drug action, chemotherapy, drug metabolism or toxicology; evolutionary mechanisms and evolutionary genetics; or clinical medicine and surgery when the contribution constitutes a substantive diagnostic, therapeutic, operative or physiological advance. Reject anyone whose principal significance lies in any other field. Reject chemists, physicists and materials scientists whose work lacks a central biological or medical application; descriptive natural historians, systematists, taxonomists and palaeontologists without a substantive experimental, genetic, physiological or mechanistic contribution; ecologists, conservationists and environmental advocates whose principal contribution concerns ecosystems, conservation or policy rather than organismal or biomedical mechanisms; agriculturists, horticulturists and veterinary practitioners whose importance lies in husbandry, breeding practice or production rather than biological science; psychoanalysts, psychotherapists and clinical psychologists without a substantive biological, neurological or physiological discovery; anthropologists, archaeologists and scholars of the sociology, history or ethics of medicine whose contributions are interpretive rather than biomedical; hospital administrators, medical politicians, health-policy officials and pharmacists whose significance is primarily institutional, regulatory or commercial rather than scientific; mathematicians, statisticians, computer scientists and bioinformaticians whose importance is principally methodological and who made no substantive biological or medical discovery; engineers whose principal contribution is device construction or industrial technology rather than biological or medical understanding; and entrepreneurs, executives or advocates whose historical importance is primarily commercial, organizational or political.",
    "name": "life sciences and medicine"
  }
